== M1: step acceleration of duration t (matter at distance r sees it only after r/c) ==
  ok   F(t)/(G rho a) = 2 pi c^2 t^2 for t < R/c: it depends on the DURATION t, not on the size of a 
  ok   m_i(t)/m_i(inf) = (c t/R_c)^2 for t < R_c/c (then 1): d/da of the ratio is identically 0 (a-independent) 
  ok   kernel K(tau) = 2 tau/T^2 (weight ~ 4 pi r dr for the 1/r kernel) is normalised 
  ok   first moment M1 = (2/3) T = (2/3) R_c/c for the sharp uniform ball 
  ok   record's action requirement M1 = (2/3) c/a0  <=>  T = c/a0, i.e. R_c = c^2/a0 (the RINDLER distance of a0) for the sharp 1/r kernel 
  ok   Yukawa-screened kernel (weight r e^{-r/R} dr): M1 = 2 T, so the identification would be T = c/(3 a0), R_c = c^2/(3 a0): shape-dependent cutoff 
  ctrl-ok  mutation: a 1/r^2 kernel (weight dr) would give M1 = T/2, not (2/3) T 
  ok   at the puzzle's cutoff R_c = c^2/a0 = 2 R* the Sciama integral is I = |Phi|/c^2 = 8 pi (closure would need 1) 
  ok   R_c(puzzle)/R_S(Sciama closure) = sqrt(8 pi) = 5.013;  R_c(puzzle)/R_H = Z = 5.789 (R_H = c/H_L) 
  ctrl-ok  mutation: the puzzle's cutoff equals the Sciama closure radius 

== M2: oscillating acceleration a cos(omega t), sharp ball of radius R_c ==
  ok   in-phase (inertial) part of the retarded response: Re chi(kR) = 2 (cos kR + kR sin kR - 1)/(kR)^2 
  ok   kR -> 0: Re chi = 1 - (kR)^2/4 + ...  (inertia is FULL at low frequency and DROPS at high frequency) 
   first zero of the in-phase inertia: kR_0 = 2.33112237041  (root of cos x + x sin x = 1; transcendental equation)
  ok   the in-phase inertia changes sign (negative effective mass) at kR_0 = 2.3311... 
  ok   Yukawa variant: Re chi_Y = (1 - (kR)^2)/(1 + (kR)^2)^2, zero exactly at kR = 1 (omega = c/R) 
  ctrl-ok  mutation: in-phase inertia is enhanced (>1) at some finite kR in the sharp model 

== M3: matter behind the accelerated observer's horizon removed (premise) ==
  ok   removed potential  int_cap dV/r = pi (R - d)^2  (exact solid-angle integral) 
  ok   mu_cap(a) = 1 - (1 - c^2/(a R))^2/2 for a > c^2/R; -> 1/2 as a -> infinity; d mu/d a < 0 (inertia falls at HIGH acceleration) 
  ok   mu_cap never falls below 1/2: no deep-MOND slope mu ~ a/a_c, and the direction is opposite to Milgrom's mu(a/a0) -> a/a0 at low a 
  ctrl-ok  mutation: mu_cap -> 0 as a -> infinity 

== M4: from a cutoff (frequency or length) to an acceleration ==
   a_c = (v/c) kR_c c^2/R_c :  the cutoff is a frequency, so the acceleration it corresponds to carries a factor v/c (velocity dependent).
   Galactic v/c ~ 1e-3: a_c ~ 1e-3 x (c^2/R_c) x kR_c, i.e. about 1e-3 a0 for R_c = c^2/a0: the retarded-integral cutoff acts far below a0 at v << c.
  ok   a_c depends on v: two orbits with the same acceleration a = v^2/r but different (v, r) have different omega = v/r, hence different retarded response (not a function of a alone) 
   Rindler conversion a_c = c^2/R_c (v = c: the Unruh/Rindler identification, a premise):
     R_c = R_H   : a_c^2/(G rho_L c^2) = 8*pi/3 = 8.37758
     R_c = R_S   : a_c^2/(G rho_L c^2) = 2*pi = 6.28319
     R_c = R*    : a_c^2/(G rho_L c^2) = 1 = 1.00000
     R_c = 2R*   : a_c^2/(G rho_L c^2) = 1/4 = 0.25000
     R_c = Z R_H : a_c^2/(G rho_L c^2) = 1/4 = 0.25000
  ok   Rindler conversion values: R_H -> 8 pi/3, R_S -> 2 pi, R* -> 1, 2R* -> 1/4 (the puzzle), Z R_H -> 1/4 
  ok   the puzzle's a0 is reproduced by the Sciama-type kernel ONLY if its cutoff is c^2/a0 = 2 R* = Z R_H = 5.789 Hubble radii, which is not the Hubble radius (=event horizon in dS), not the Sciama closure radius, not R* 
   needed q: R_c = q R_H -> q = 4*sqrt(6)*sqrt(pi)/3;  R_c = q R_S -> q = 2*sqrt(2)*sqrt(pi);  R_c = q R* -> q = 2
  ok   R_c = q R* needs q = 2 (rational, but R* is not a physical horizon: it is c/sqrt(G rho_L), the dimensional-analysis length); R_c = q R_H needs q = Z (transcendental), R_c = q R_S needs q = sqrt(8 pi) 
   => the only cutoff of the form (rational) x (a length built from G rho_L alone) that works is R* itself with q = 2; R* is the length the puzzle is about, so this is the puzzle restated.

== M5: closure + Rindler cutoff with a general gravito-electric coupling factor g (Sciama's coefficient 1 is a premise) ==
  ok   closure I = 1 with the kernel cutoff at the Rindler distance c^2/a0 gives a0^2/(G rho c^2) = 2 pi g 
  ok   the puzzle (a0^2/(G rho c^2) = 1/4) needs g = 1/(8 pi): the coupling of the vector potential would have to be G/(8 pi) 
  ok   no rational g reproduces it (pi-class): 2 pi g = 1/4 needs g irrational; the fixed cutoff R_c = c^2/a0 is the only place a0 enters 
  ctrl-ok  mutation: g = 1 (Sciama) reproduces 1/4 

